{{Navbar-Global Gas Infrastructure Tracker}}
'''Equitrans Gas Pipeline''' is an operating natural gas pipeline in the United States.<ref name="r30800f">{{Cite web|url=https://www.eia.gov/naturalgas/pipelines/EIA-NaturalGasPipelineProjectsAug2026.xlsx|title=Natural Gas Pipeline Projects, August 2026 release, U.S. Energy Information Administration|website=eia.gov|access-date=2026-11-04}}</ref>

==Location==
The pipeline runs from Wetzel County, West Virginia to Greene County, Pennsylvania.<ref name="r1459f5">{{Cite web|url=https://regulations.justia.com/regulations/fedreg/2011/05/18/2011-12118.html|title=regulations.justia.com|website=regulations.justia.com|access-date=2026-11-04}}</ref>

==Project Details==
*'''Owner: '''Equitrans LP<ref name="r1459f5" />
*'''Capacity: '''313.56 MMcf/d<ref name="r30800f" />
*'''Length: '''44.4 mi<ref name="r1459f5" />
*'''Diameter: '''16, 24 in<ref name="r1459f5" />
*'''Status: '''Operating<ref name="r30800f" />
*'''Start year: '''2012<ref name="r30800f" />
*'''Cost: '''US$272,000,000<ref name="r30800f" />

==Background==
Equitrans, L.P. proposed the Sunrise Pipeline Project in Federal Energy Regulatory Commission docket CP11-68. In a notice published May 18, 2011, FERC staff said the project would also include 2.6 miles of replacement pipe, the uprating of 4.8 miles of existing pipe, and a new compressor station in Jefferson Township, Greene County, Pennsylvania. It listed five meter station interconnects, 12 mainline block valves and four pig launchers and receivers. Comments on the environmental assessment were due June 10, 2011.<ref name="r1459f5" />

The U.S. Energy Information Administration lists the project as completed on July 19, 2012, at a cost of $272 million. It gives the length as 50 miles, while the 2011 notice gave 44.4 miles of new pipe.<ref name="r30800f" />

